# THE LITERAL TYPE IS NOT A TYPE A PROGRAM CAN WRITE. `intliteral` is what the
# checker gives a lexeme before context pins it, so a parameter declared as one
# can never be satisfied: an argument either arrives already pinned, or coerces
# at the bind site. `core` does not re-export the literal types, which is what
# keeps them off a program's surface.
#
# THE MESSAGE IS POOR AND THIS PINS IT SO IT CANNOT DRIFT UNNOTICED. The bare
# name resolves to a SHELL row that also carries the spelling `intliteral`, so
# the mismatch reads "expected intliteral, got intliteral" -- two different ids
# with one name. The clean answer is E0023 "Unknown type", which is what a name
# `core` does not re-export gets everywhere else; the shell fallback is general
# machinery and its own fix.
takesLit: function {n: intliteral} out i64 is {
    return n.i64
}

main: function is {
    print "r=\{takesLit n: 5}"
}
